subsection.p:
       matrix, addition 
       matrix, scalar multiplication 
      This means that we may define scalar multiplication and matrix
      addition operations using the corresponding column-wise vector
      operations.
      For instance,

	\begin{aligned}
	c\left[\begin{array}{rrrr}
	\vec{v_1} \amp \vec{v_2} \amp \cdots \amp \vec{v_n}
	\end{array}
	\right]
	{}={} \amp
	\left[\begin{array}{rrrr}
	c\vec{v_1} \amp c\vec{v_2} \amp \cdots \amp c\vec{v_n}
	\end{array}
	\right] \\
	\left[\begin{array}{rrrr}
	\vec{v_1} \amp \vec{v_2} \amp \cdots \amp \vec{v_n}
	\end{array}
	\right]
	{}+{} \amp
	\left[\begin{array}{rrrr}
	\vec{w_1} \amp \vec{w_2} \amp \cdots \amp \vec{w_n}
	\end{array}
	\right]
	 \\
	{}={} \amp
	\left[\begin{array}{rrrr}
	\vec{v_1}+\vec{w_1} \amp \vec{v_2}+\vec{w_2} \amp \cdots \amp
	\vec{v_n}+\vec{w_n} 
	\end{array}
	\right]. \\
	\end{aligned}

statement.p:
Suppose we have the matrix A and vector \vec{x}:
A = \left[\begin{array}{rr} -2 \amp 3 \\ 0 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp 1 \\ \end{array}\right],~~~ \vec{x} = \left[\begin{array}{r} 2 \\ 3 \\ \end{array}\right] . Their product will be defined to be the linear combination of the columns of A using the components of \vec{x} as weights. This means that
\begin{aligned} A\vec{x} = \left[\begin{array}{rr} -2 \amp 3 \\ 0 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp 1 \\ \end{array}\right] \left[\begin{array}{r} 2 \\ 3 \\ \end{array}\right] {}={} \amp 2 \left[\begin{array}{r} -2 \\ 0 \\ 3 \\ \end{array}\right] + 3 \left[\begin{array}{r} 3 \\ 2 \\ 1 \\ \end{array}\right] \\ \\ {}={} \amp \left[\begin{array}{r} -4 \\ 0 \\ 6 \\ \end{array}\right] + \left[\begin{array}{r} 9 \\ 6 \\ 3 \\ \end{array}\right] \\ \\ {}={} \amp \left[\begin{array}{r} 5 \\ 6 \\ 9 \\ \end{array}\right]. \\ \end{aligned}

The product of a matrix A by a vector
\vec{x} will be the linear combination of the columns of
A using the components of \vec{x} as weights.
More specifically, if

  A=\left[\begin{array}{rrrr}
  \vec{v_1} \amp \vec{v_2} \amp \ldots \amp \vec{v_n}
  \end{array}\right],~~~
  \vec{x} = \left[\begin{array}{r}
  c_1 \\ c_2 \\ \vdots \\ c_n \end{array}\right],

then

  A\vec{x} = c_1\vec{v_1} + c_2\vec{v_2} + \ldots + c_n\vec{v_n}
  .

If A is an m\times n matrix, then \vec{x}
must be an n-dimensional vector, and the product
A\vec{x} will be an m-dimensional vector.

li.p:
We have

  \begin{alignedat}{2}
  \left[\begin{array}{rrrr}
  1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp -2 \\
  2 \amp 4 \amp -3 \amp -2 \\
  -1 \amp -2 \amp 6 \amp 1 \\
  \end{array}\right]
  \amp
  \fourvec{3}{1}{-1}{1} \\
  \amp =
  \threevec{3(1)+1(2)-1(0)+1(-1)}
  {3(2)+1(4)-1(-3)+1(-2)}
  {3(-1)+1(-2)-1(6)+1(1)} \\
  \amp =\threevec{4}{11}{10}\text{.}
  \end{alignedat}

If A\vec{x}=\vec{b}, then we have
\begin{alignedat}{3} x_1 \amp {}+{} \amp 2x_2 \amp {}={} \amp 6 \\ -x_1 \amp {}+{} \amp x_2 \amp {}={} \amp 0\text{.} \\ \end{alignedat}
with corresponding augmented matrix
\left[\begin{array}{rr|r} 1 \amp 2 \amp 6 \\ -1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \end{array}\right] \sim \left[\begin{array}{rr|r} 1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \end{array}\right]\text{.}
This means that \vec{x}=\twovec{2}{2} is the unique solution to the equation A\vec{x}=\vec{b}.

subsection.p:
      To see the connection between the matrix equation A\vec{x} =
      \vec{b} and linear systems, let's write the matrix A in
      terms of its columns \vec{v_i} and \vec{x} in terms of
      its components.

	A = \left[
	\begin{array}{rrrr}
	\vec{v_1} \amp \vec{v_2} \amp \ldots \vec{v_n}
	\end{array}
	\right],
	\vec{x} = \left[
	\begin{array}{c}
	c_1 \\ c_2 \\ \vdots \\ c_n \\
	\end{array}
	\right]
      .

statement.p:
 If A=\left[\begin{array}{rrrr}
	\vec{v_1}\amp\vec{v_2}\amp\ldots\vec{v_n}
	\end{array}\right] and \vec{x}=\left[
	\begin{array}{c}
	x_1 \\ x_2 \\ \vdots \\ x_n \\
	\end{array}\right]
	, then the following statements are equivalent.



 The vector \vec{x} satisfies the equation A\vec{x} = \vec{b}
	  .  



 The vector \vec{b} is a linear combination of
	  the columns of A with weights x_j:

	      x_1\vec{v_1} + x_2\vec{v_2} + \ldots + x_n\vec{v_n} = \vec{b}
	  . 



 The components of \vec{x} form a solution to
	  the linear system corresponding to the augmented matrix

	    \left[\begin{array}{rrrr|r}
	    \vec{v_1} \amp \vec{v_2} \amp \cdots \amp \vec{v_n} \amp \vec{b}
	    \end{array}\right]\text{.}

li.p:
Consider the linear system
\begin{alignedat}{4} 2x \amp {}+{} \amp y \amp {}-{} \amp 3z \amp {}={} \amp 4 \\ -x \amp {}+{} \amp 2y \amp {}+{} \amp z \amp {}={} \amp 3 \\ 3x \amp {}-{} \amp y \amp \amp \amp {}={} \amp -4. \\ \end{alignedat}
Identify the matrix A and vector \vec{b} to express this system in the form A\vec{x} = \vec{b}.

 If A and \vec{b} are as below, write the
	linear system corresponding to the equation
	A\vec{x}=\vec{b} and describe its solution space, using a
	parametric description if appropriate:

	  A = \left[\begin{array}{rrr}
	  3 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
	  -2 \amp 0 \amp 6
	  \end{array}
	  \right],~~~
	  \vec{b} = \left[\begin{array}{r}
	  -6 \\ 2
	  \end{array}
	  \right].

Describe the solution space of the equation

  \left[
  \begin{array}{rrrr}
  1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\
  2 \amp 4 \amp -3 \amp -2 \\
  -1 \amp -2 \amp 6 \amp 1 \\
  \end{array}
  \right]
  \vec{x}
  = 
  \left[\begin{array}{r}
  -1 \\ 1 \\ 5
  \end{array}
  \right]
.

 Suppose A is an m\times n matrix.  What
	can you guarantee about the solution space of the equation A\vec{x}
	= \vec{0}?

We have the augmented matrix
\left[\begin{array}{rrrrr} 1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp -1 \\ 2 \amp 4 \amp -3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp -2 \amp 6 \amp 1 \amp 5 \end{array}\right] \sim \left[\begin{array}{rrrrr} 1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{array}\right]\text{.}
Since this system is inconsistent, there are no solutions to the matrix equation.

statement.p:
 Given matrices A and B, we form their
	product AB by first writing B in terms of its
	columns

	  B = \left[\begin{array}{rrrr}
	  \vec{v_1} \amp \vec{v_2} \amp \cdots \amp \vec{v_p}
	  \end{array}\right]

	and then defining

	  AB = \left[\begin{array}{rrrr}
	  A\vec{v_1} \amp A\vec{v_2} \amp \cdots \amp A\vec{v_p}
	  \end{array}\right].
